TITLE: CyberAgent: An Agentic AI Framework for Autonomous Security Operations with Reduced Operational Cost
TEXT: Volume 21, 2026 CYBERAGENT: AN AGENTIC AI FRAMEWORK FOR AUTONOMOUS SECURITY OPERATIONS WITH REDUCED OPERATIONAL COST Sasivimol Meeampol Narongsak Sukma* * Corresponding author Faculty of Business Administration, fbussas@ku.ac.th Kasetsart University, Bangkok, Thailand Faculty of Digital, Siam Technology College, Bangkok, Thailand narongsaks@siamtechno.ac.th ABSTRACT Aim/Purpose Background Methodology Contribution The primary objective is to address the structural operational cost crisis in modern SOCs by designing and formally specifying a three-layer agentic AI framework that integrates semantic alert triage, adaptive reinforcement-learning response, and episodic knowledge synthesis into a unified architecture. Modern SOCs are experiencing an acute operational crisis. Exponential growth in alert volume, high false-positive rates (>40%), and chronic analyst attrition have created a perfect storm. Existing rule-based SIEM and single-agent SOAR approaches only achieve 20–55% alert automation and fail to address the full operational lifecycle. The framework evaluation is based on a structured comparison with seven benchmark systems across four dimensions (threat coverage breadth, integration completeness, cost quantification, and adversarial safeguards), each rated on a five-level ordinal scale using replicable criteria. The paper makes four contributions: (1) a formal algorithmic specification of a three-layer agentic AI architecture including three pseudocode procedures and a PPO state-action-reward formalism; (2) a quantitative operational cost projection framework explicitly distinguishing designed targets from measured performance, with a maximum designed workload reduction of 86%; (3) a systematic four-dimensional comparative analysis against seven benchmark frameworks Accepting Editor Geoffrey Z. Liu │ Received: April 30, 2026 │ Revised: July 1, July 8, July 13, 2026 │ Accepted: July 19, 2026. Cite as: Meeampol, S., & Sukma, N. (2026). CyberAgent: An agentic AI framework for autonomous security operations with reduced operational cost. Interdisciplinary Journal of Information, Knowledge, and Management, 21, Article 15. https://doi.org/10.28945/5852 (CC BY-NC 4.0) This article is licensed to you under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International License. When you copy and redistribute this paper in full or in part, you need to provide proper attribution to it to ensure that others can later locate this work (and to ensure that others do not accuse you of plagiarism). You may (and we encourage you to) adapt, remix, transform, and build upon the material for any non-commercial purposes. This license does not permit you to use this material for commercial purposes. CyberAgent Findings Recommendations for Practitioners Recommendations for Researchers Impact on Society Future Research Keywords demonstrating that CyberAgent is the only framework achieving full architectural completeness (integrating all three of semantic triage, adaptive RL response, and episodic knowledge synthesis simultaneously), an architectural claim requiring empirical confirmation; and (4) dual adversarial safeguards (prompt injection mitigation and reasoning consistency verification) absent from all seven benchmark frameworks. CyberAgent is a theoretical design-science artefact that has neither been implemented nor empirically evaluated. All quantitative projections are designed to achieve targets grounded in prior work, not verified outcomes: an alert automation rate of 85–90%, an analyst workload reduction of 56–86% (design target 86% under the product-rule independence assumption), an MTTR reduction of 65–75%, and a false-positive reduction of 60–70%. These projections require empirical validation using CybORG and the DARPA OpTC dataset, with this as the primary future work priority. The PTL’s Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning traces provide human-readable decision narratives that enhance transparency and may support auditability workflows relevant to GDPR Article 33, HIPAA, PCI-DSS, and SOX. However, CoT traces do not automatically satisfy regulatory auditability or compliance requirements; they are one architectural input to a broader compliance process. Formal legal and compliance assessment by qualified professionals is required before deployment in regulated environments. Practitioners should treat CoT output as decision-support documentation, not as regulatory certification. Future work should also develop federated DRL training protocols to ensure policy convergence under data-scarce conditions and rigorously test the adversarial robustness of the PTL’s consistency-checking mechanism against novel prompt-injection strategies. If empirically validated, CyberAgent could make enterprise-grade cyber defence more accessible to mid-market organisations that cannot afford 24/7 SOC analyst staffing, by substantially reducing the alert-triage workload. All such impact claims are conditional on validation results. Priority directions include: (1) empirical implementation and red-team validation across diverse enterprise environments; (2) federated DRL training to address the data sharing constraints that limit policy learning in regulated sectors; (3) extension of the KSL to support cross-organizational threat intelligence sharing; and (4) longitudinal studies measuring analyst skill development and human-AI trust calibration under progressively increasing levels of CyberAgent autonomy. agentic AI, autonomous cyber defence, deep reinforcement learning, large language models, security operations centre, operational cost reduction INTRODUCTION Security Operations Centers (SOCs) face an escalating volume of alerts, ranging from 10,000 to 100,000 events per day. Yet, no existing autonomous defence framework integrates semantic triage, adaptive response, and episodic learning into a single operational lifecycle with cost reduction as an explicit design objective. This paper introduces CyberAgent, a cooperative three-layer multi-agent architecture comprising a Perception and Triage Layer (PTL) that performs Chain-of-Thought seman- 2 Meeampol, & Sukma tic alert classification, a Decision and Response Layer (DRL-Layer) that selects hierarchical countermeasures via Proximal Policy Optimisation, and a Knowledge Synthesis Layer (KSL) that maintains an episodic incident graph for continual learning. Using a Design Science Research methodology, CyberAgent is formally specified through pseudocode algorithms and evaluated ex-ante against seven benchmark frameworks across four comparative dimensions, with a quantitative cost-reduction projection grounded in published empirical benchmarks and a sensitivity analysis characterising projection’s robustness. The comparative analysis indicates that no reviewed framework achieves architectural completeness across all three SOC lifecycle capabilities, while CyberAgent’s projected combined analyst workload reduction is derived from published PTL and DRL-Layer coverage benchmarks. CyberAgent contributes a unified, empirically groundable design artefact for SOC automation research and an explicit agenda for the empirical validation required before operational deployment. Srinivas et al. (2025) report that the average enterprise Security Operations Center (SOC) processes between 10,000 and 100,000 alerts per day, an existential operational challenge driven by the proliferation of connected devices, the adoption of cloud services, and the increasing sophistication of adversarial tooling. Analyst teams can manually investigate only a small fraction of these alerts, resulting in alert fatigue that severely degrades analyst judgement and drives SOC underperformance and analyst attrition (Forsberg & Frantti, 2023; Srinivas et al., 2025). Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR) tools have partially addressed this volume problem. However, Bridges et al. (2023) demonstrate that the automation ceiling for point-solution SOAR deployments is typically 40– 55% of alert types, leaving the majority of triage, investigation, and escalation decisions as predominantly manual analyst effort. The operating gap carries serious economic and human consequences. Ask et al. (2021) confirm that alert fatigue directly impairs cognitive performance in analyst teams. Forsberg and Frantti (2023) report that analyst labour accounts for 60–70% of SOC operating expenditure, with costs driven mainly by time-to-triage, time-to-contain, and false-positive investigations. Bernier et al. (2012) classify cost drivers in defensive cyber operations as MTTR, analyst utilisation, and automation ratio. Reinforcement learning agents CO-DECYBER (Cheah, Stone, Haubrick, et al., 2024), ADIF (Omar et al., 2025), RADAR (Ebrahimi et al., 2025), and LLM-based agentic frameworks ACIR (Baral et al., 2025) and IDSO (Feng et al., 2025) provide credible technical pathways to substantially higher automation rates. Organisational adoption of cybersecurity automation is further shaped by structural investment determinants such as governance maturity, perceived risk, and resource allocation (Rattanapong et al., 2025). Despite these advances, no framework identified in the seven studies reviewed integrates these capabilities into a coherent architecture addressing the full operational lifecycle – from alert ingestion through triage, response, escalation, and continual learning – with operational cost reduction as an explicit primary design objective. The seven studies were selected through a systematic scoping procedure: candidate frameworks were identified by searching IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, and Google Scholar using the terms “autonomous SOC,” “agentic AI cybersecurity,” “deep reinforcement learning cyber defence,” and “LLM security automation,” restricted to peer-reviewed publications from 2024 to 2026. Inclusion criteria were: (a) autonomous or semi-autonomous decision-making in a SOC context; (b) at least one of the three target capabilities (semantic triage, adaptive response, or episodic learning); and (c) sufficient architectural description for independent analysis. This selection process is a structured scoping review, not a full systematic review, and other qualifying architectures may exist in the broader literature. This synthesis gap motivates the three-layer CyberAgent architecture. The decomposition is grounded in the distinct inductive biases of three model classes: transformer-based LLMs for openvocabulary semantic alert classification, where alternative classifiers such as gradient-boosted trees require fixed feature vocabularies that cannot generalise across heterogeneous alert formats; PPOclass RL agents for stable policy gradient optimisation under non-stationary reward distributions, where rule-based SOAR cannot adapt to novel attack variants without manual re-engineering; and 3 CyberAgent attributed graph architectures for encoding typed multi-hop relationships between events, where flat feature vectors cannot represent campaign-level causal structure. These capabilities are complementary rather than competing, and no single model class optimally satisfies all three requirements simultaneously (Alneyadi & Normalini, 2023; Magunje et al., 2025). Importantly, the claim that no such unified architecture exists is bound to the seven reviewed frameworks; other architectures may exist in the broader literature. Xenos and Serpanos (2026) define Agentic AI as a goal-oriented artificial intelligence system that autonomously perceives structured information from its environment, maintains an internal state model, reasons over multi-step sequences of actions, and performs goal-directed actions over long operational horizons without requiring per-decision human authorisation. This paradigm distinguishes CyberAgent from static classifiers and traditional SOAR automation and serves as the theoretical foundation for the framework. This paper makes four contributions. First, it formally specifies CyberAgent through pseudocode algorithms for all three core computational procedures. Second, it provides a quantitative operational cost projection framework grounded in published empirical benchmarks, with all projections explicitly labelled as designed to achieve targets that require empirical validation. Third, it offers a systematic four-dimensional comparative analysis against seven benchmark frameworks using operationalised, replicable criteria. Fourth, it presents a parameter sensitivity analysis characterising projection robustness under degraded assumptions. This is an ex-ante formative Design Science Research evaluation per Hevner et al. (2004) and Venable et al. (2016); all performance projections must be empirically validated, which is identified as the primary future work priority. RELATED WORK AND LITERATURE REVIEW EXISTING CYBER DEFENSE FRAMEWORKS We define architectural completeness as the integration of all three core SOC lifecycle capabilities – semantic triage, adaptive response, and episodic continual learning – into a single, unified data flow where the output of each layer feeds live into the input of the others. By this criterion, no framework identified in our review achieves architectural completeness: RADAR (Ebrahimi et al., 2025) provides adversarially robust DRL response, but neither semantic triage nor episodic learning; IDSO (Feng et al., 2025) provides LLM-based triage, but neither adaptive RL response nor episodic memory; ADIF (Omar et al., 2025) provides hierarchical RL with federated knowledge distribution, but not LLM semantic triage. CyberAgent is designed to fulfil all three simultaneously. The seven frameworks were selected because they represent the most recent peer-reviewed work on autonomous SOC automation (2024–2026); this is not a systematic review, and other architectures may exist in the broader literature. Table 1 summarises each framework’s primary domain focus, key capabilities, identified gaps, type of evidence (empirical vs. projected), and explicit justification for inclusion, providing a scholarly basis for the selection and addressing potential study selection bias. Chandola et al. (2009) established the taxonomic basis for anomaly detection, identifying the fundamental limitation of single-event analysis for detecting distributed multi-stage attack campaigns—a limitation addressed by KSL’s incident graph architecture. Guarino (2013) provided an early theoretical framing of autonomous intelligent agents in cyber operations. Onwubiko (2015) developed an operational model for a cybersecurity operations centre monitoring. Cheah, Stone, Haubrick, et al. (2024) applied deep multi-agent RL to cyber-physical OT environments and later generalised to cooperative autonomous cyber defence (Cheah, Stone, Bailey, et al., 2024). Omar et al. (2025) introduced ADIF using hierarchical reinforcement learning with federated knowledge distribution. Ebrahimi et al. (2025) published RADAR in MIS Quarterly, establishing adversarial robustness as a non-negotiable prerequisite for production DRL-based cyber defence. CyberAgent builds directly on RADAR’s reward-poisoning detection and extends it with LLM-based semantic triage, episodic incident graph memory, and operational cost as a primary reward signal component (see “Decision and 4 Meeampol, & Sukma Response Layer (DRL-Layer)” below, where the reward function R(s, a, s′) is specified). Primary challenges identified for the field remain scalability, adversarial robustness, and cost efficiency (Dehghantanha, Parizi, & Epiphaniou, 2024; Dehghantanha, Yazdinejad, & Parizi, 2024). Verification-based anomaly detection, such as one-time-key issuance schemes for detecting caller ID spoofing, offers a complementary identity-verification strategy to event-pattern anomaly detection (Sukma & Chokngamwong, 2017, 2018). REINFORCEMENT LEARNING FOR ADAPTIVE TH REAT RESPONSE Nguyen and Reddi (2023) surveyed DRL for cybersecurity and found that PPO is the most preferred algorithm for network-based cyber defence due to its stability under non-stationary reward distributions. Loevenich et al. (2025) conducted an empirical evaluation of a hybrid DRL–LLM autonomous cyber defence agent, confirming the technical feasibility of the architecture that CyberAgent extends to the full SOC lifecycle. Duclos et al. (2025) assessed RL for cyberattack mitigation and reported comparative PPO performance data. Huang and Zhu (2019) provided theoretical foundations for adversarial robustness mechanisms. Terry et al. (2021) introduced the PettingZoo standard API for multi-agent RL, enabling systematic comparison of cooperative architectures. Three integration gaps remain: (1) no prior work combines DRL response and LLM triage within a single unified policy loop; (2) reward function designs treat cost reduction as implicit rather than incorporating analyst labour as an explicit training signal; and (3) episodic continual learning across completed incidents has not been implemented in any prior DRL framework, including RADAR. LARGE LANGUAGE MODELS AND AGENTIC AI FOR SECURITY AU T OM AT ION Srinivas et al. (2025) present a comprehensive survey of LLMs in SOC automation, citing siloed point-solution integration as the primary limitation and reporting that AI-augmented SOC deployments achieve a 60–90% reduction in workload compared to fully manual operations. Feng et al. (2025) introduced IDSO, demonstrating more than 80% reduction in task completion time—the primary empirical anchor for CyberAgent’s triage automation rate projection. Baral et al. (2025) developed ACIR, demonstrating LLM-based autonomous incident handling at production alert volumes using the ReAct reasoning paradigm. Xenos and Serpanos (2026) formalised agentic AI as a distinct paradigm for cyber-physical systems cybersecurity. Alecsoiu et al. (2025) demonstrated that agentic AI automation can reduce operational costs by more than 52.7% in e-commerce, providing a crossdomain cost benchmark. Mariaraj et al. (2025) showed that intelligent meta-controller routing for LLM inference reduces computational load by 68%, confirming the economic viability of LLM-based alert triage at enterprise scale. None of these approaches addresses the full operational lifecycle with unified episodic knowledge retention. The integration gap that CyberAgent is designed to address (Arambepola et al., 2025; Munir et al., 2025). KNOWLEDGE SYNTHESIS AND MULTI-AGENT SYSTEMS Dawoud et al. (2023) developed goal-driven decision support for cyber operations under incomplete information, defining episodic knowledge requirements for autonomous cyber systems. Almeaibed et al. (2021) demonstrated digital twin-based operational modelling; KSL’s dynamic infrastructure state representation is informed by this work. Fedorov (2025) proposed AI-powered multi-agent systems for real-time security analysis of software releases. Li et al. (2025) enhanced optimisation algorithms for alarm data fusion. These contributions treat knowledge synthesis as a distinct sub-system rather than a live episodic infrastructure feeding directly into real-time triage and response decisions – the gap that CyberAgent’s KSL is designed to fill. Al Maqousi et al. (2025) demonstrated autonomous cyber defence in smart city settings; Alauthman, Al-Qerem, et al. (2025) and Kim et al. (2021) tackled vehicle network security; Ureche et al. (2025) provided guidelines for AI microservice deployment; Rahal et al. (2025) applied federated learning to EV charging station security. The adversarial context motivating CyberAgent’s prompt injection mitigation is informed by Guembe et al. (2022) and Wazid et al. (2022). Falowo and Bou Abdo (2026) empirically demonstrated that incremental deployment 5 CyberAgent and rigorous decision auditability are prerequisites for high automation readiness in production SOC environments. The literature also reveals several under-addressed topics across existing frameworks: data poisoning beyond reward manipulation, model drift under distribution shift, unsafe tool use by autonomous agents, enforcement of access control policies, and structured recovery from incorrect autonomous actions. These represent open research challenges that CyberAgent acknowledges but does not fully resolve in the current design artefact. THE CYBERAGENT FRAMEWORK CyberAgent is developed within the Design Science Research (DSR) methodology (Hevner et al., 2004), in which the framework is treated as a theoretical design artefact whose practical value is evaluated through structured ex-ante comparison with benchmark systems, and whose implementation feasibility is supported by proven component-level performance from prior work. It has not been implemented or empirically evaluated; all performance claims are projections requiring validation. OVERALL ARCH ITECTURE CyberAgent is a cooperative multi-agent system comprising three layers. The Perception and Triage Layer (PTL) handles alert ingestion and semantic classification; the Decision and Response Layer (DRL-Layer) selects adaptive countermeasures via reinforcement learning; and the Knowledge Synthesis Layer (KSL) maintains episodic memory and continual learning infrastructure. All three communicate via a common message bus that transports Structured Intelligence Objects (SIOs); normalised JSON Schema representations of threat incidents, contextual annotations, response outcomes, and performance metrics. This unified representation is intended to address the cross-layer information flow gap that Srinivas et al. (2025) identify as the critical missing element in current point-solution SOC automation (Figure 1). The three-layer decomposition is theoretically motivated by the complementary inductive biases of each model class. Transformer-based LLMs provide open-vocabulary generalisation for classifying semantically diverse alert text, a capability that gradient-boosted trees cannot match across heterogeneous alert formats. PPO-class RL agents offer stable policy gradient optimisation under non-stationary reward distributions; rule-based SOAR, by contrast, cannot adapt to novel attack variants without manual re-engineering. Attributed graph architectures contribute relational inductive bias for encoding typed multi-hop relationships between events. These capabilities are complementary, not competing, and no single model class optimally satisfies all three requirements simultaneously. This justification is theoretical; empirical ablation studies comparing alternative architectures are required to confirm it. Scope: CyberAgent targets enterprise IT SOC environments processing heterogeneous SIEM alert streams. The PTL’s natural language reasoning is designed to generalise across diverse alert formats without domain-specific retraining. DRL-Layer policy, however, requires fine-tuning on domain-representative incident data before deployment in operational technology (OT) or critical infrastructure (CI) environments—additional validation steps that are outside the scope of this design artefact. 6 Meeampol, & Sukma consistency_fail escalation Layer 1: Perception & Triage Layer (PTL) Layer 2: Decision & Response Layer (DRL-Layer) Layer 3: Knowledge Synthesis Layer (KSL) SOAR.execute() ESCALATE SIO SIO SIO + outcome Shared Message Bus — Structured Intelligence Objects (SIOs) Figure 1. CyberAgent three-layer cooperative architecture PERCEPTION AND TRIAGE LAYER (PTL) The PTL is the front-end interface to the security event stream for CyberAgent. Inspired by the ReAct agent paradigm of Baral et al. (2025) and the intention-understanding approach of Feng et al. (2025), the PTL takes raw SIEM alerts as input and outputs a Structured Intelligence Object containing: a threat classification label from MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise v15; a severity score in [0, 1]; a chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning narrative; and a recommended response priority from {Critical, High, Medium, Low, Benign}. The LLM reasoning module applies a five-step structured CoT template: (1) Event Identification; (2) Pattern Matching against MITRE ATT&CK; (3) Contextual Enrichment using KSL data; (4) Threat Hypothesis Generation with confidence estimates; and (5) Triage Decision. False-positive reduction follows the contextual enrichment approach of Omar et al. (2025): each raw event is augmented with asset criticality, threat intelligence feeds, and historical patterns prior to reasoning. Adversarial robustness is addressed via two mechanisms following Ebrahimi et al. (2025) and Guembe et al. (2022). Prompt injection is mitigated through a parameterised template that separates event data from reasoning instructions. Reasoning consistency verification runs each alert through two independent LLM instances with randomized temperatures; semantic inconsistency (cosine similarity < 0.70 between generated threat hypotheses) is treated as a trigger for automatic human escalation, consistent with Reimers and Gurevych (2019). This 0.70 threshold is a design parameter that requires calibration against an organisational validation set based on the precision-recall tradeoff specific to the deployment environment; the appropriate value will differ across organisations depending on their risk tolerance and false-negative costs. In contrast to CyberAgent’s design-parameter approach, Prakash et al. (2026) empirically validated a comparable multi-layer semantic detection framework on a labelled prompt-injection dataset (n = 662), achieving accuracy and F1 of 0.974, and demonstrated that cosine similarity thresholds in security-sensitive LLM tasks require calibration against the deployment-specific precision-recall tradeoff rather than adoption from general NLP benchmarks. Dynamic calibration using held-out incident data is therefore a required future work priority. The dual-instance consistency check mitigates adversarial manipulation but does not overcome the base LLM classification limitations; triage accuracy remains bounded by the underlying language model’s capabilities and requires empirical benchmarking. The complete PTL procedure is formally specified in Algorithm 1 and illustrated in Figure 2. 7 CyberAgent Phase 1 — Event Normalization Phase 2 — Five-Step Structured Chain-of-Thought Reasoning Phase 3 — Dual-Instance Adversarial Consistency Check YES Phase 4 — SIO Assembly & Emission NO (sim ≥ 0.7) Figure 2. PTL Chain-of-Thought triage dataflow Algorithm 1. PTL Chain-of-Thought Triage Procedure Algorithm 1: PTL — Chain-of-Thought Triage Procedure Input: e = raw SIEM alert {event_type, timestamp, src_ip, dst_ip, severity_raw, metadata} C = KSL context {asset_criticality, threat_intel, historical_patterns} Output: SIO = Structured Intelligence Object with CoT trace and triage decision 1: // Phase 1 — Event Normalization 2: e_norm ← normalize(e) → {event_type, timestamp, src_asset, severity_raw} 3: C ← KSL.query(e_norm.src_asset, e_norm.event_type, window=30d) 4: // Phase 2 — Five-Step Structured CoT Reasoning 5: step1 ← LLM.identify(e_norm) + source profile // CoT-1: Event class 6: step2 ← LLM.match(step1, MITRE_ATT&CK) // CoT-2: Technique code identification 7: step3 ← LLM.enrich(step2, C) threat intel augmentation // CoT-3: Asset + 8: step4 ← LLM.hypothesize(step3) confidence c ∈ [0,1] // CoT-4: Threat type, 9: step5 ← LLM.decide(step4) {Critical,High,Med,Low,Benign} // CoT-5: Priority ∈ 8 Meeampol, & Sukma Algorithm 1: PTL — Chain-of-Thought Triage Procedure 10: // Phase 3 — Dual-Instance Adversarial Consistency Check 11: h₁ ← LLM_instance_1(e_norm, C, τ₁) perature τ₁ // instance 1 at tem- 12: h₂ ← LLM_instance_2(e_norm, C, τ₂≠τ₁) // instance 2 at temperature τ₂ 13: sim ← cosine_similarity(embed(h₁), embed(h₂)) 14: if sim < 0.7 then 15: return SIO(priority=ESCALATE, reason="consistency_fail", trace=[h₁,h₂]) 16: end if 17: // Phase 4 — SIO Assembly and Emission 18: SIO ← {event_id, priority=step5, severity=step4.c, threat_type=step4.type, MITRE_codes=step2.codes, CoT_trace=[step1,…,step5], timestamp} 19: message_bus.emit(SIO) 20: return SIO ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── Complexity: O(L) per alert, where L = number of LLM inference calls (minimum 12 for the 5-step CoT chain plus dual-instance consistency check); each individual LLM call is O(1) in alert count but the total per-alert computational cost scales with L and with token length. Dominant cost = 2× LLM inference per dual-instance consistency check; meta-controller routing reduces effective L for low-ambiguity alerts. DECISION AND RESPONSE LAYER (DRL-LAYER) The DRL-Layer is a reinforcement learning agent based on the Proximal Policy Optimisation (PPO) algorithm. The policy network maps threat state representations to hierarchical response actions. The state space S is a structured feature vector concatenating: (a) the SIO from the PTL (severity score, classification label, CoT-derived threat hypothesis embedding); (b) the KSL environment model snapshot (asset criticality profile, network topology adjacency matrix, active vulnerability count); and (c) the incident graph GNN embedding for the current episode. The action space A is hierarchical, following the cooperative decomposition of Cheah, Stone, Haubrick, et al. (2024): high-level actions Aᴴ = {Isolate, Contain, Remediate, Monitor, Escalate} and low-level actions Aᴸ(aᴴ) specify the particular SOAR playbook for each high-level action. The reward function R(s, a, s′) = α · r_containment(s, a, s′) − β · r_latency(a) − γ · r_disruption(s, a, s′) directly incorporates the operational cost objective into the RL training signal, consistent with Bernier et al. (2012). Terms are defined as follows: r_containment ∈ [0,1] measures the degree to which the selected action successfully contains or neutralises the identified threat; r_latency ∈ [0,1] is a normalised penalty for 9 CyberAgent the time elapsed from alert ingestion to SOAR execution; r_disruption ∈ [0,1] penalises collateral disruption to legitimate services caused by the response action; and α, β, γ ∈ (0,1) are weighting coefficients summing to 1.0. Default coefficients (α = 0.6, β = 0.2, γ = 0.2) are author-defined design choices consistent with the SOC priority ordering reported by Forsberg and Frantti (2023), who identify containment as the primary performance metric; however, Forsberg and Frantti (2023) do not explicitly state these coefficient values, and the numerical values are the authors’ operationalisation of that priority ordering. Practitioners should recalibrate these coefficients based on their organisation’s specific security and operational objectives. This linear scalarisation assumes cardinal comparability of objectives. Future empirical iterations will explore Pareto-front multi-objective RL (Roijers et al., 2013); the Pareto frontier is expected to be concave due to diminishing marginal returns to the emphasis on containment beyond α ≈ 0.70, though this expectation requires empirical confirmation. For cases where action confidence falls below τ_esc = 0.70, CyberAgent follows a human escalation pathway consistent with Falowo and Bou Abdo (2026). Reward poisoning detection applies the Grubbs test to a rolling window of k = 100 reward observations; the normality assumption is defensible at k = 100 by the Central Limit Theorem, with an IQR-based fallback during policy transition periods. Algorithm 2 describes the complete DRL-Layer procedure. Algorithm 2. DRL-Layer PPO Hierarchical Response Selection Algorithm 2: DRL-Layer — PPO Hierarchical Response Selection Input: SIO = Structured Intelligence Object from PTL π_θ = policy network (actor-critic, PPO parameters θ) τ_esc = escalation confidence threshold (default 0.70) Output: a = response action (a_H, a_L); θ’ = updated policy parameters ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── 1: // Phase 1 — State Vector Construction 2: s_ptl ← embed(SIO.severity, SIO.threat_type, SIO.MITRE_codes) 3: s_ksl ← KSL.snapshot() vuln count // asset criticality, topology, 4: s_graph ← KSL.graph_embed(SIO.event_id) // GNN embedding of incident graph 5: s ∈ ℝᵈ ← concat(s_ptl, s_ksl, s_graph) // full state vector s 6: // Phase 2 — Hierarchical Action Selection 7: a_H_dist ← π_H(s; θ) // P(a_H | s) over A_H={Isolate,Contain,Remediate,Monitor,Escalate} 8: a_H ← argmax(a_H_dist) 9: conf_H ← max(a_H_dist) 10: if conf_H < τ_esc then 11: return a = ESCALATE, θ’ = θ lation // no policy update on esca- 10 Meeampol, & Sukma Algorithm 2: DRL-Layer — PPO Hierarchical Response Selection 12: end if 13: a_L ← argmax(π_L(s, a_H; θ)) call // specific SOAR playbook 14: a ← (a_H, a_L) 15: // Phase 3 — Execution, Reward, and Replay 16: SOAR.execute(a_L) 17: s’ ← KSL.snapshot() action // observe next state post- 18: r ← α·r_contain(s,a,s’) − β·r_latency(a) − γ·r_disrupt(s,a,s’) 19: // α + β + γ = 1; α=0.6, β=0.2, γ=0.2 (default) 20: replay_buffer.store(s, a, r, s’) 21: // Phase 4 — PPO Policy Update (every N=1000 steps) 22: if replay_buffer.size() mod N == 0 then 23: θ’ ← PPO_update(replay_buffer, θ, epochs=10, clip_ra- tio=0.2) 24: else 25: θ’ ← θ 26: end if 27: KSL.update_registry(SIO.threat_type, s, a, r, latency=SOAR.exec_time) 28: return a, θ’ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── Complexity: Inference O(d²); PPO update O(N·d²·epochs) amortized every N steps KNOWLEDGE SYNTHESIS LAYER (KSL) The KSL provides episodic memory and a continual learning infrastructure, improving performance over time. Inspired by the federated knowledge distribution of Omar et al. (2025) and the digital twin-inspired operational modelling of Almeaibed et al. (2021), the KSL comprises three persistent knowledge structures: (1) an incident graph G = (V, E, F), a directed attributed graph where nodes V represent security events, edges E encode causal and temporal relationships (temporal-sequence, colocation, tool-sharing, campaign-membership), and feature vectors F encode semantic descriptions derived from PTL SIOs; (2) a response outcome registry that stores action-outcome pairs for each resolved incident, enabling continual policy improvement; and (3) an environment model M_env encoding the dynamic state of the defended infrastructure including asset criticality profiles, network topology, and active vulnerability inventory. The temporal edge detection window W = 72 hours is set based on empirical attacker dwell-time data (Mandiant, 2024; median dwell time of 10 days, with initial exploitation-to-lateral-movement phases typically falling within 24–72 hours). W is a tunable hyperparameter that requires SOC-specific calibration; threat actors with longer operational horizons require a larger W, which increases 11 CyberAgent graph size and lookup cost. The campaign-membership cosine similarity threshold θ_c = 0.75 is a design parameter set above the PTL consistency threshold of 0.70 to enforce stricter semantic agreement for the higher-stakes campaign-association decision. Like the PTL cosine threshold, θ_c requires empirical calibration against a labelled incident validation set to balance false campaign links against missed multi-stage detections. Increasing θ_c improves precision at the cost of recall; the tradeoff should be evaluated against the organisation’s threat model and acceptable false-negative risk. The incident graph is designed to enable the PTL to discover multi-stage attack campaigns across seemingly unrelated events, directly addressing the fundamental limitation documented by Chandola et al. (2009). The full KSL update procedure is given in Algorithm 3 and Figure 3. Ontology-based alignment frameworks that formally structure heterogeneous knowledge domains (Namahoot et al., 2025) provide a methodological precedent for the KSL’s structured, typed representation of incident knowledge. Figure 3. KSL incident graph G = (V, E, F) structure Algorithm 3. KSL Incident Graph Update Procedure Algorithm 3: KSL — Incident Graph Update Procedure Input: SIO = Structured Intelligence Object (from PTL or DRLLayer) o = response outcome {action, result, latency} G = current incident graph (V, E, F); M_env = environment model W = temporal edge detection window (default 72h) θ_c = campaign-membership similarity threshold (default 0.75) Output: G’ = (V’, E’, F’), updated M_env’, reward-poisoning flag f_rp 12 Meeampol, & Sukma Algorithm 3: KSL — Incident Graph Update Procedure ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── 1: // Phase 1 — New Node Creation 2: v_new ← {event_type: SIO.threat_type, timestamp: SIO.ts, src_asset: SIO.src, severity: SIO.severity, MITRE: SIO.codes} 3: F_new ← LLM_encoder(SIO.CoT_trace) // semantic feature vector ∈ ℝᵏ 4: V’ ← V ∪ {v_new}; F’ ← F ∪ {F_new} 5: // Phase 2 — Multi-Type Edge Detection (Temporal Window W) 6: for each v_u ∈ V with |v_new.ts − v_u.ts| ≤ W do 7: if v_new.src_asset == v_u.src_asset then 8: E’ ← E’ ∪ {(v_u, v_new, type=‘co-location’)} 9: end if 10: if |v_new.ts − v_u.ts| < Δt_seq then poral-sequence // tem- 11: E’ ← E’ ∪ {(v_u, v_new, type=‘temporal-sequence’)} 12: end if 13: if same_tactic_group(v_new.MITRE, v_u.MITRE) then // tool- sharing 14: E’ ← E’ ∪ {(v_u, v_new, type=‘tool-sharing’)} 15: end if 16: if cosine_similarity(F_new, F_u) > θ_c then paign-membership // cam- 17: E’ ← E’ ∪ {(v_u, v_new, type=‘campaign-membership’)} 18: end if 19: end for 20: // Phase 3 — Environment Model and Registry Update 21: M_env’ ← M_env.update(v_new.src_asset, v_new.severity, o.result) 22: outcome_registry.append(SIO.threat_type, s, o.action, o.result, o.latency) 23: // Phase 4 — Reward Poisoning Detection 24: r_window ← outcome_registry.rolling(k=100) 25: f_rp ← detect_anomaly(r_window.rewards) // Grubbs test on rolling distribution 26: if f_rp then 27: flag_human_review("Reward poisoning suspected; policy up- date suspended") 13 CyberAgent Algorithm 3: KSL — Incident Graph Update Procedure 28: end if 29: G’ ← (V’, E’, F’) 30: KSL_api.notify(PTL, DRL_Layer, event="graph_updated") 31: return G’, M_env’, f_rp ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── ───── Complexity: O(|V|·d) per event for edge scan; O(1) amortized with time-windowed index OPERATIONAL COST REDUCTION, TCO, AND LATENCY MANAGEMENT Four projected cost-reduction mechanisms drive CyberAgent’s TCO case, each a designed-to-achieve target requiring empirical validation rather than a measured outcome. Automated PTL triage is projected to handle up to 90% of alerts autonomously at 50,000 alerts per day, reducing human triage decisions by up to 45,000 per day. Sub-3-second DRL-Layer execution is designed to reduce MTTR. KSL-driven continual playbook learning is projected to improve response accuracy over time. Finally, reduced alert fatigue through automation is expected to lower analyst attrition – a mechanism empirically confirmed by Ask et al. (2021). Table 5 provides a step-by-step LLM call breakdown for CyberAgent versus a SOAR-only baseline. For a routine alert, CyberAgent requires 12 LLM calls (5 CoT steps + 2 dual-instance consistency + 5 KSL enrichment lookups), compared to 0 for rule-based SOAR; high-severity escalation alerts require 14–18 calls depending on KSL graph depth. The SOC automation scope in this paper is deliberately bounded to alert triage and adaptive response. Compliance audit, structured escalation workflows, infrastructure monitoring, model tuning, inference cost management, and rollback procedures are outside the current design artefact and represent required extensions for full SOC deployment. API inference costs at current rates of approximately USD 0.001–0.01 per call, with a minimum of 12 calls per alert, imply USD 600–6,000 per day for 50,000 alerts. That is substantially lower than the estimated USD 4–8 million annual analyst labour costs for a typical 20–40 analyst SOC team, but both figures depend on stable API pricing and achieving the projected automation rate, neither of which is guaranteed. Computational latency is a major constraint. A 5-step CoT chain, combined with dual-instance consistency checking, requires at least 12 sequential LLM inference calls per alert; at enterprise alert volumes, such sequential processing would breach SOC time-to-triage requirements. CyberAgent proposes mitigating this by using the intelligent meta-controller routing of Mariaraj et al. (2025), which dynamically routes simpler alerts to low-latency lightweight models and reserves computationally intensive LLMs for ambiguous, high-severity events, thereby achieving a projected 68% reduction in computational load. This routing approach requires empirical benchmarking under production alert velocities before its viability can be confirmed. Notation summary across all algorithms: d = state vector dimensionality; N = PPO update interval in steps (default 1,000); k = rolling reward window size (default 100); |V| = incident graph node count; W = temporal edge detection window (default 72h). This projection is consistent with empirical evidence that structured, analytics-driven log management can materially reduce IT operational costs (Sukma et al., 2019). 14 Meeampol, & Sukma COMPARATIVE ANALYSIS METHODOLOGY DESIGN SCIENCE RESEARCH EVALUATION FRAMEWORK The evaluation applies a structured comparative analysis methodology based on the DSR guidelines of Hevner et al. (2004). Since CyberAgent is a design artefact, its evaluation uses the ex-ante formative evaluation framework proposed by Venable et al. (2016). The comparison set comprises seven peer-reviewed academic frameworks: CO-DECYBER (Cheah, Stone, Haubrick, et al., 2024), ADIF (Omar et al., 2025), ACIR (Baral et al., 2025), IDSO (Feng et al., 2025), AI-Augmented SOC (Srinivas et al., 2025), Autonomous Critical Infrastructure Defence (Alauthman, Al-Qerem et al., 2025), and RADAR (Ebrahimi et al., 2025). RADAR is selected as the primary RL benchmark due to its rigorous mathematical treatment of adversarial robustness. The seven frameworks were selected as the most recent peer-reviewed work on autonomous SOC automation (2024–2026); no competing architectures combining all three capabilities were identified in this set, though such architectures may exist elsewhere in the literature. Commercial platforms (Microsoft Sentinel with Copilot, CrowdStrike Falcon, Palo Alto Cortex XSIAM) are excluded due to insufficient architectural transparency for independent replication under DSR norms (Figure 4). A — DSR Evaluation Approach B — Four Evaluation Dimensions C — 5-Level Ordinal Scale D — Comparison Set: Seven Peer-Reviewed Benchmark Frameworks Framework CO-DECYBER ADIF ACIR IDSO AI-Aug. SOC Autonomous CID RADAR ★ E — Architectural Completeness Gap Analysis: All 7 Benchmarks vs. CyberAgent Semantic Triage (LLM) ✗ Adaptive RL Respo nse ✓ Episodic Learning (KSL) ✗ Arch. Complete? Partial ✗ ✓ ✗ Partial ✓ ✗ ✗ Partial ✓ ✗ ✗ Partial ✓ ✓ ✗ Partial ✗ ✓ ✗ Partial ✗ ✓ ✗ Partial ★ CyberAgent (proposed) ✓ ✓ ✓ FULL ✓ Gap Count (of 3) ■ ■ □ (2 gaps) ■ ■ □ (2 gaps) ■ ■ □ (2 gaps) ■ ■ □ (2 gaps) ■ □ □ (1 gap) ■ ■ □ (2 gaps) ■ ■ □ (2 gaps) □ □ □ (0 gaps) F — Evidence Types & Future Empirical Validation Protocol Figure 4. Comparative analysis evaluation framework EVALUATION DIMENSIONS AND RANKING CRITERIA Each framework is assessed on four dimensions using a five-point ordinal scale {Low, Medium-Low, Medium, Medium-High, High} based on the following operationalised criteria. Threat Coverage Breadth: Low = 1 attack vector or domain; Medium = 2–3 attack types; Medium-High = broad IT/OT coverage across 4+ attack types; High = cross-domain coverage (IT, OT, cloud) with adaptive domain conditioning. Automation Depth: Low = <30% alert types automated; Medium = 30– 55% (SOAR level); Medium-High = 55–75%; High = >75% end-to-end including triage, response, and continual learning. Operational Cost Reduction: assessed as a percentage reduction in analyst labour hours compared to fully manual SOC operations. Architectural Completeness: Partial = at least one of {semantic triage, adaptive response, episodic learning} absent from a unified data flow; Full = 15 CyberAgent all three integrated. All seven framework scores are taken directly from primary source texts; scores based on author projections are noted in the Table 1 footnotes. CyberAgent’s scores reflect designed capabilities, not measured outcomes, and should be read accordingly. PROJECTED WORKLOAD REDUCTION: MATH EMATICAL BASIS AND INDEPENDENCE ASSUMPTION The projected combined analyst workload reduction by CyberAgent is calculated as the product of the PTL automation rate (85–90%, based on Feng et al., 2025) and the DRL-Layer response coverage rate (95% of PTL-triaged critical alerts, based on Loevenich et al., 2025). The design target under the product rule is 90% × 95% = 85.5%, which we round to 86%. The minimum conservative projection (PTL = 70%, DRL-Layer = 80%) yields a combined workload reduction of 56%. The full range is therefore 56–86%. This product rule assumes statistical independence between PTL classification errors and DRL-Layer failures. The assumption is structurally defensible when PTL errors arise from semantic ambiguity in LLM classification and DRL-Layer failures arise from limited training data for novel attack variants, as these have distinct causal drivers. However, the assumption may be violated under zero-day attacks that are simultaneously semantically ambiguous and policy-novel, causing positive correlation ρ between failure modes and leading to overestimation. The sensitivity analysis in Table 4 characterises degradation under correlated failure. Even the conservative lower bound of 56% exceeds the empirically established SOAR-only ceiling of 40–55% (Srinivas et al., 2025), validating the conservative bound as plausible against the empirical floor. FUTURE EMPIRICAL EVALUATION ENVIRONMENTS Phase 1 empirical validation will use the CybORG simulation environment (CAGE Challenge 4 variant) and the DARPA OpTC dataset, which provides realistic multi-stage APT scenarios for RL policy training and adversarial red-team evaluation. The evaluation protocol comprises: (a) five standardised multi-stage attack campaigns from CAGE Challenge 4; (b) metrics operationalised as MTTR (seconds from SIEM alert ingestion to SOAR execution confirmation), alert automation rate (percentage of alerts resolved without human action within 60 seconds), false-positive rate (percentage of alerts confirmed as non-threats in post-incident review), and analyst workload reduction (labour hours per 1,000 incidents compared to SOAR-only baseline); and (c) statistical comparison using paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests against the SOAR-only baseline, with Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons. RESULTS M ULTI-DIMENSIONAL FRAMEWORK COMPARISON The structured comparative assessment is presented in Table 1. A critical methodological distinction must be maintained throughout: cost reduction figures for CO-DECYBER (Cheah, Stone, Haubrick, et al., 2024) and ADIF (Omar et al., 2025) are author projections from architectural capability descriptions; the only directly measured empirical figures are those for IDSO (Feng et al., 2025; >80%) and the AI-Augmented SOC range (Srinivas et al., 2025; 60–90%). CyberAgent’s scores reflect designed capabilities and designed-to-achieve targets, not measured outcomes. All comparisons should be interpreted accordingly. OPERATIONAL WORKFLOW SCENARIO ANALYSIS Table 2 illustrates how CyberAgent would respond to a common multi-stage incident—a port scan escalating to a lateral movement campaign. This scenario is a conceptual walkthrough demonstrating intended architectural behaviour under realistic conditions; it is not an empirical result. The walkthrough illustrates how the three-layer architecture functions in a realistic incident context with practical latency constraints. 16 Meeampol, & Sukma Table 1. Structured comparative assessment of autonomous cyber defense frameworks, including k