and does not generalise to enterprise IT without retraining; ADIF (Omar et al., 2025) addresses general IT security but lacks domain-specific adaptation mechanisms. Second, capability incompleteness: RADAR (Ebrahimi et al., 2025) provides the most rigorous treatment of adversarial robustness but operates at training time and lacks LLM-based semantic triage and episodic learning at deployment time. None of the seven benchmark frameworks addresses cross-domain applicability for smart city, vehicle network, and telecommunications contexts in a single unified architecture. CyberAgent addresses domain fragmentation through two architectural features. The PTL’s LLM reasoning module processes natural language event representations, enabling it to process alerts from heterogeneous environments without domain-specific retraining. The KSL’s environment model encodes domain context as a learnable variable, conditioning the DRL-Layer policy on domain-specific characteristics. We do not claim zero retraining for cross-domain transfer; the DRL-Layer policy still requires domain-specific fine-tuning. However, the PTL and KSL are designed to reduce most domain-adaptation overhead. These claims require empirical validation to confirm. Table 2. CyberAgent operational workflow: Port scan to lateral movement campaign scenario Stage Time CyberAgent Action (Layer) Outcome Analyst 1 T+0s PTL: Ingests SIEM alert SIO created; KSL None (SYN scan, external IP, 18 queried for source IP internal ports) history and asset criticality 2 T+2s PTL CoT (Alg. 1): (1) Severity=Critical; None Identify: port scan; (2) Match: triage trace logged; MITRE T1046; (3) Enrich: SIO passed to DRL- AD server in scope, IP not in Layer allowlist; (4) Hypothesis: Reconnaissance conf=0.84; (5) Decision: Critical. Meta- controller routes to lightweight model (low ambiguity alert). 3 T+3s DRL-Layer (Alg. 2): AD Containment None server criticality=0.95; executed in <3s; KSL conf_H=0.91 > τ_esc=0.70; outcome registry selects Contain → Block updated external IP via SOAR API 4 T+45m KSL (Alg. 3): New internal Campaign SIO None scan shares campaign- created; DRL-Layer membership edge notified (cosine_sim=0.81 > θ_c=0.75) with T+0 event; PTL detects MITRE T1021 lateral movement 19 CyberAgent Stage Time CyberAgent Action (Layer) Outcome Analyst 5 T+46m DRL-Layer: conf_H=0.61 < Analyst notified with Analyst reviews τ_esc=0.70 (novel lateral full SIO chain, CoT and authorizes movement variant); Escalate traces, incident graph response action selected view playbook Note. SIO = Structured Intelligence Object; CoT = Chain of Thought; SOAR = Security Orchestration, Automation and Response; AD = Active Directory; MITRE ATT&CK technique codes cited. Algorithm step references in parentheses. Response time benchmarks are consistent with Loevenich et al. (2025). This scenario illustrates intended architectural behaviour and does not represent measured performance. QUANTITATIVE COST REDUCTION ANALYSIS With CyberAgent’s intended architectural advantages established, we now translate those structural features into projected quantitative operational outcomes. All CyberAgent figures in Tables 3 and 4 are designed to achieve targets grounded in the empirical benchmarks of Feng et al. (2025) and Srinivas et al. (2025); none has been empirically validated. Table 3 presents projected cost reduction metrics across the automation spectrum. Figure 5 and Table 4 present the parameter sensitivity analysis for the combined workload-reduction estimate, with the design target in the lower-right cell. Table 3. Projected quantitative operational cost reduction CybeAgent vs. benchmark systems Framework Alert auto. rate (%) MTTR reduction (%) False positive reduction (%) Analyst workload reduction (%) Evidence Rule-Based SIEM 20–30 <10 (Baseline) 10–20 10–20 Empirical¹ SOAR-only 40–55 25–40 20–35 30–45 Empirical¹ CO-DECYBER ~60 ~35 ~40 ~40–60 Projected² ADIF ~65 ~45 ~50 ~50–65 Projected² IDSO / LLM >80 >60 >55 >80 Empirical³ (Feng et al., 2025) AI-Aug. SOC (Srinivas et al., 2025) 60–90 40–70 40–65 60–90 Empirical range³ CyberAgent (proposed) 85–90 65–75 60–70 56–86 Designed⁴ ¹ Empirically reported from Bridges et al. (2023) and Srinivas et al. (2025). ² Author projections from architectural capability descriptions; not empirically measured. ³ Directly reported empirical measurements. ⁴ Designed-to-achieve targets grounded in Feng et al. (2025) and Srinivas et al. (2025). Analyst workload reduction range 56–86% reflects sensitivity analysis bounds (Table 4); design target is 86% (PTL = 90%, DRL-Layer = 95%). All values require empirical validation. 20 Meeampol, & Sukma Figure 5. Operational cost reduction comparison Table 4. Sensitivity analysis: Combined analyst workload reduction (%) under PTL × DRL-layer coverage scenarios DRL-layer/ PTL coverage PTL = 70% PTL = 80% PTL = 90% (baseline) DRL-Layer = 80% 56% 64% 72% DRL-Layer = 90% 63% 72% 81% DRL-Layer = 95% 67% 76% 86% ★ design target Note. Combined workload reduction = PTL coverage × DRL-Layer coverage (product rule; independence assump- tion). ★ Design target (PTL = 90%, DRL-Layer = 95%): 85.5% ≈ 86%. Independence assumption and its limitations are discussed in “Projected Workload Reduction: Mathematical Basis and Independence Assumption” below. All values are designed-to-achieve projections requiring empirical validation. The maximum achievable reduction under this formula is 86%. DISCUSSION TH EORETICAL IMPLICATIONS Three theoretical contributions emerge from CyberAgent’s design. The first is the operationalisation of agentic AI as the governing paradigm for end-to-end SOC automation – a direction explicitly called for by Xenos and Serpanos (2026). Prior frameworks have employed individual agentic components: LLM reasoning (Baral et al., 2025; Feng et al., 2025), RL-based action (Cheah, Stone, Haubrick, et al., 2024; Ebrahimi et al., 2025; Loevenich et al., 2025), or knowledge synthesis (Omar et al., 2025). None of the seven reviewed studies, however, combines all three into a single live data flow spanning perception, reasoning, planning, action, and episodic memory. That is the synthesis gap. RADAR (Ebrahimi et al., 2025), the most rigorous prior RL work, addresses adversarial robustness 21 CyberAgent at training time yet lacks LLM-based semantic triage and deployment-time episodic learning, confirming the gap is real. This claim is bounded to the reviewed literature and should not be generalised beyond it. The second contribution is embedding operational cost as a first-class design criterion: cost reduction appears explicitly in the formal reward signal and drives the TCO projection framework, rather than being an incidental benefit. Alecsoiu et al. (2025) demonstrated a>52.7% reduction in operational costs through agentic AI in e-commerce; whether comparable reductions are achievable in SOC environments remains an open empirical question. The third contribution is more speculative: the threelayer decomposition principle may generalise beyond SOC automation to autonomous vulnerability management, continuous compliance monitoring, and adaptive network segmentation – a conjecture warranting evaluation across those domains. CyberAgent supports phased adoption, but the following roadmap is a research-based planning framework, not an implementation guide. Each phase requires pilot testing, safety verification, rollback planning, and clear stopping criteria before progression (Figure 6). Phase 1 (Months 1–3): PTL deployed as a stand-alone triage assistant connected to the SIEM, with a conservative escalation threshold (τ_esc = 0.90) to maximise human oversight. All PTL decisions are reviewed by analysts; no autonomous response actions are taken. Phase 2 (Months 4–6): DRL-Layer enabled for low-risk automated responses (Monitor and Contain actions only), with comprehensive logging and analyst review of all decisions. Phase 3 (Months 7+): Incremental refinement of escalation thresholds bas